# Two-anchor geometry and fusion.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR
	bool "Two-anchor door geometry and inside/outside fusion"
	help
	  Door swing angle from the frame-to-leaf UWB distance (ultrawidelock_door.h), and
	  the side-of-door verdict plus triangle-inequality consistency gate over
	  two anchors' distances to one phone (ultrawidelock_fusion.h).

	  Integer arithmetic over caller-owned structs: no allocation, no threads,
	  no static state, and no platform dependency. The whole module is about
	  1 KB of flash and zero bytes of RAM until a caller declares a struct.

	  Default n, and nothing selects it yet. The logic was written and
	  host-tested ahead of the hardware it describes, so turning it on is a
	  separate decision that comes with a measured RAM budget -- the
	  DWM3001CDK Matter image has roughly 6 KB free and a new static
	  allocation there is a decision rather than a detail.

	  Also carries ultrawidelock_side.h: the fail-closed INSIDE/OUTSIDE/THRESHOLD/
	  UNKNOWN gate for passive approach unlock. That gate is separate from
	  ultrawidelock_fusion_may_predict() and must be selected explicitly via
	  CONFIG_ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE before it can suppress a passive unlock.

	  Both halves carry a MOUNTING requirement that no code can substitute
	  for: the two anchors must sit equidistant from the hinge (ultrawidelock_door),
	  and symmetrically about the door plane (ultrawidelock_fusion). Read those headers
	  before drilling anything.

if ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	bool "Fail-closed side gate for passive approach unlock"
	default n
	help
	  When y, UNLOCK_PREDICT and UNLOCK_THRESHOLD from ultrawidelock_approach are
	  released only if ultrawidelock_side_may_passive_unlock() is true: side must be
	  confident OUTSIDE with fresh evidence, quorum, and no contradiction.

	  UNKNOWN, INSIDE, THRESHOLD, stale, replayed, multi-phone, and
	  degraded evidence all suppress passive unlock. NFC Express Mode,
	  Apple Home commands, and mechanical operation are not gated here.

	  Default n preserves today's behaviour. Turning this on without
	  commissioned BLE witnesses will refuse every passive unlock -- that
	  is intentional fail-closed behaviour, not a regression.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_OUTSIDE_MARGIN_DB
	int "RSSI margin to call OUTSIDE, decibels"
	default 6
	range 1 20
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	help
	  How much louder the outside witness must hear the phone than the
	  inside one before a window classifies as OUTSIDE (symmetrically,
	  INSIDE). Below the margin the window is dead-band and cannot commit
	  a side. 6 dB assumes the door itself sits between the witnesses; a
	  bench with both dongles in open air on one plane rarely reaches it,
	  so bench overlays lower this. Lowering it on a mounted door trades
	  inside-safety for availability -- the margin is the whole defence
	  against multipath reading an inside phone as outside.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_PEER_EMIT
	bool "Bench: log the credential peer address at L2CAP CoC open"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	default n
	help
	  Logs `SIDE peer=<AdvA> type=<public|random>` when the credential L2CAP CoC
	  opens and `SIDE peer=clear` when it closes, so bench tooling can push
	  the phone's current address to the BLE witnesses as an advertising
	  filter. Without it a witness summarises every advertiser in the room.

	  That address is personal data. A resolvable private address identifies
	  whoever is standing at the door for as long as it lasts, so this is
	  default n and must stay off in a shipped image. It is a bench aid, not
	  a product feature: the witnesses need a way to learn which advertiser
	  is the credential, and this is the temporary one.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_FEED_RTT
	bool "Bench: ingest SF1 witness summaries over SEGGER RTT down-buffer"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	depends on USE_SEGGER_RTT
	default n
	help
	  Parses ASCII lines:
	    SF1 in=<dbm> out=<dbm> th=<dbm> ni=<n> no=<n> nt=<n>
	  from RTT down-buffer 0 into ultrawidelock_side_filter_feed().

	  Bench only: it needs a debug probe holding the RTT connection, so it is
	  not a path a deployed lock can use. It used to default y, which meant
	  selecting the gate silently selected a J-Link dependency. Default n now,
	  and the SIDE overlay turns it on explicitly.

	  This is currently the ONLY transport into the gate. Until a witness
	  link over UART or Matter exists, ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE without this option
	  refuses every passive unlock, exactly as its own help warns.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_CODEC
	bool "BLE witness wire format, window accumulator and phone picker"
	default n
	help
	  The three platform-free pieces both ends of the witness link share:
	  the WV2 report codec, the per-window accumulator that decides which
	  advertisers a witness reports, and the trajectory correlator that
	  picks which of them is the phone.

	  Selected by ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH on the lock, and set directly
	  by the witness firmware, which links the same code without wanting
	  the lock's side gate. Two implementations of one wire format agree
	  until they do not, so there is only one.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	bool "Inside veto: refuse passive unlock for a phone believed indoors"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	select ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_CODEC
	default n
	help
	  Adds ultrawidelock_latch.h on top of the side gate. The side gate asks
	  "which side is the phone on right now" every window and needs a
	  confident OUTSIDE to release a grant. MEASURED 2026-08-11, that
	  question is unanswerable where it matters: 49% of windows landed in
	  the dead band at the door plane, which is exactly where the approach
	  controller asks.

	  The latch changes the question. Each credential carries a persistent
	  INSIDE belief whose resting state is INSIDE and which no amount of
	  missing evidence can move. Clearing it takes a live session, a
	  recorded crossing opportunity, and a run of agreeing OUTSIDE windows
	  that STARTS beyond ULTRAWIDELOCK_LATCH_CLEAR_MIN_MM -- and the grant
	  then re-latches immediately, so nothing cleared ever persists.

	  Costs one settings key and about 1 KB of RAM. Turning this on without
	  a witness link refuses every passive unlock, exactly as the side gate
	  does: that is the intended fail-closed behaviour.

	  Read docs/inside-latch.md before changing any default below.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK
	bool
	help
	  The sealed peer link itself: one UDP socket on the Thread interface,
	  AES-CCM under a per-peer key, a monotonic counter per peer boot, and a
	  challenge nonce. It carries a VERSION-TAGGED message and knows nothing
	  about what any version means.

	  Selected, never set directly. It exists so a consumer can use the
	  transport without inheriting another consumer's device class: the second
	  UWB anchor rides this link and has nothing to do with BLE witnesses,
	  which are retired. Without this split, enabling the anchor would compile
	  in witness ingest and enrol the anchor's key as though it were a witness.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_LINK
	bool "Ingest second-anchor distance reports (WV3) over the sealed link"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR
	depends on NET_L2_OPENTHREAD || OPENTHREAD
	select ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK
	select ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_MSG
	default n
	help
	  Accepts sealed WV3 reports from a second UWB anchor: its own measured
	  distance to the phone, and the ranging block it measured it in.

	  Its own key and its own replay window, NOT a witness role slot. The
	  anchor is a UWB responder sharing the credential session's keys, not a
	  BLE observer, and enrolling it through the witness path would tie a live
	  device class to a retired one.

	  The block travels with the distance because a distance without the round
	  it belongs to cannot be paired with this node's own.

	  NOT YET STANDALONE. The anchor's identity, numeric KV key, enrolment
	  command and replay window are all its own, and the advertiser picker is
	  witness-only. But witness_link.c still contains the WV2 consumer inline,
	  woven through the nonce and health paths, so an image with ANCHOR_LINK and
	  no WITNESS_LINK_OT does not link yet. Splitting that application transport
	  is a separate refactor; every shipping config enables both.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_LINK_OT
	bool "Ingest BLE witness reports over Thread UDP"
	select ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	depends on NET_L2_OPENTHREAD || OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Binds a UDP socket on the Thread interface and decodes sealed WV2
	  datagrams from the enrolled BLE witnesses, replacing the RTT SF1 feed
	  that needed a debug probe held open for the session.

	  Each report is AES-CCM sealed under a per-witness key, carries a
	  monotonic counter per witness boot, and echoes the lock's current
	  challenge nonce. Only reports echoing the CURRENT nonce may clear the
	  inside veto; a replayed genuine "phone is outside" window is the one
	  forgery that would open a door, so the clear direction is challenged
	  and the veto direction is not.

	  The witnesses hold no authority. Every rule is enforced here, and the
	  worst a forged or flooding witness achieves is a door that does not
	  open passively.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_MAX
	int "Enrolled witnesses"
	default 3
	range 2 4
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK
	help
	  Two is the working set: inside and outside. Three leaves room for the
	  optional threshold role without a rebuild.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_NONCE_MS
	int "Challenge nonce lifetime, milliseconds"
	default 30000
	range 1000 600000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK
	help
	  How long one challenge epoch lasts while a credential session is
	  live. Shorter narrows the replay window and costs downlink traffic to
	  sleepy witnesses; the witnesses poll at roughly 500 ms, so anything
	  below a couple of seconds mostly buys missed windows.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_LATCH_ENTRY_DWELL_MS
	int "Post-grant dwell before another passive unlock, milliseconds"
	default 60000
	range 1000 600000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	help
	  After any grant the phone is assumed to have gone inside, and during
	  the walk-in it is at the door plane where every classification is
	  noise. This is how long that assumption is held unconditionally.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_LATCH_CLEAR_WINDOWS
	int "Agreeing OUTSIDE windows required to clear the veto"
	default 3
	range 1 20
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	help
	  Matches ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE's own agree_windows, and runs in
	  series with it: these are windows the side filter had already
	  committed. One would make a single misread sufficient.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_LATCH_CLEAR_MIN_MM
	int "Range at which a clear must begin, millimetres"
	default 3000
	range 500 20000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	help
	  The FIRST agreeing window must be taken at or beyond this range, so
	  every clear starts far from the door where the two witnesses are not
	  close to equidistant and the sign of the differential means what it
	  says. Later windows may be closer; that is the approach happening.

	  UNMEASURED as a distance. It comes from the geometry argument in
	  ultrawidelock_fusion.h, not from a capture.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_LATCH_CLEAR_VALID_MS
	int "How long a proven approach stays usable, milliseconds"
	default 10000
	range 1000 120000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_INSIDE_LATCH
	help
	  This is what carries a proven approach across the dead band at the
	  door plane -- the job ultrawidelock_side_cfg::outside_hold_ms was
	  reaching for, as bounded evidence rather than a widened confidence
	  window. Long enough to walk the last few metres, far too short to
	  still be valid next time anyone passes the door.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_MM
	int "Distance between the two anchors, millimetres"
	default 1200
	range 100 10000
	help
	  Measured centre-to-centre between the two anchor antennas. This is the
	  one side of the phone-anchor-anchor triangle that is known, so every
	  consistency check is relative to it and a wrong value here silently
	  mis-sizes all of them.

	  Longer is better for discrimination: the dead band is a fixed number of
	  millimetres, so a longer baseline spends a smaller fraction of the
	  approach on "ambiguous". If more than 20% of approaches land in the
	  dead band, move the anchors further apart rather than shrinking the
	  band.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_2_MM
	int "Distance to the satellite in role 2, millimetres (0 = not installed)"
	default 0
	range 0 10000
	help
	  The same measurement as ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_MM, for a SECOND
	  satellite. Per role because the baseline is the distance from this node
	  to THAT board, and two boards are not in the same place: one value
	  shared between them sizes every triangle test for one and mis-sizes it
	  for the other, which reads as a satellite under attack rather than as a
	  number nobody set.

	  0 means no satellite is mounted in this role, which is the default and
	  the shipping case -- one lock, one satellite. A zero baseline is
	  already how this tree spells "no satellite" (ultrawidelock_satellite.c
	  fresh()), so an uninstalled role permits prediction exactly as a quiet
	  one does; it does not withhold.

	  ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_MM stays the role-1 value, unrenamed, so
	  a one-satellite deployment keeps the setting it already has.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_3_MM
	int "Distance to the satellite in role 3, millimetres (0 = not installed)"
	default 0
	range 0 10000
	help
	  Role 3 (threshold) baseline. See ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_2_MM;
	  3 is the ceiling, set by ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_ROLE's `range 1 3` and by
	  the sealed link's nonce split, not by this file.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_TOL_MM
	int "Triangle-inequality tolerance, millimetres"
	default 90
	range 0 1000
	help
	  Slack on both triangle tests, sized at roughly 3 sigma of the ranging
	  jitter so ordinary noise at the geometric boundary does not read as an
	  attack. 90 assumes the 30 mm jitter stage A targets; re-derive it from
	  the jitter actually measured, because a tolerance smaller than the
	  noise rejects real approaches.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DEADBAND_MM
	int "Ambiguity dead band, millimetres"
	default 60
	range 0 1000
	help
	  Distance-difference smaller than this is called UNKNOWN rather than a
	  side, because the sign is inside the noise. Roughly 2 sigma of ranging
	  jitter. A phone in the doorway is genuinely ambiguous and has to read
	  that way; UNKNOWN permits prediction, so this is a bias toward the door
	  opening.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BOUNDARY_BIAS_MM
	int "Side boundary shift toward the inside anchor, millimetres"
	default 0
	range 0 10000
	help
	  0 keeps the INSIDE/OUTSIDE frontier on the anchors' perpendicular
	  bisector, which is the door plane when the anchors straddle the door
	  -- the shipping install needs no shift. A positive value moves the
	  frontier toward the inside anchor: on the axis, INSIDE begins
	  (BASELINE_MM - this) / 2 in front of it. Meant for a bench where both
	  anchors stand on the same side of the walkway and "inside" should
	  mean "behind the lock board". Keep it several sigma of ranging jitter
	  below BASELINE_MM: at equality the INSIDE locus degenerates to the
	  ray behind the anchor and every verdict there is a coin toss.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_STALE_MS
	int "Satellite report lifetime, milliseconds"
	default 1500
	range 100 30000
	help
	  A report older than this is discarded and the verdict falls back to
	  UNKNOWN, which permits prediction. Geometry from a stale distance is
	  worse than no geometry because it looks authoritative.

	  Size it above the satellite's worst-case report interval plus its link
	  latency, or a healthy satellite will keep expiring between reports.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SELF_INSIDE
	bool "This node is the INSIDE anchor"
	default y
	help
	  Which side of the door THIS board is mounted on. A mounting fact, not a
	  code fact, and it has to be written down somewhere -- getting it
	  backwards inverts the verdict, so the node would withhold prediction
	  from people inside the house and grant it to people outside.

	  Default y because the door node carries the lock and the credential,
	  and that belongs on the protected side. Set n only if this specific
	  board is physically the outer anchor.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	bool "LIS2DH12 impact and tamper interrupt"
	depends on I2C && GPIO
	help
	  Configure the accelerometer's INT1 generator over I2C and latch its
	  edges from a GPIO callback, so the existing 250 ms loop can ask whether
	  the door was struck.

	  Does NOT use Zephyr's lis2dh sensor driver, which is already linked
	  into both shipping CDK images and stays dead. That is a RAM decision
	  rather than a preference: CONFIG_LIS2DH_TRIGGER_GLOBAL_THREAD puts an
	  ISR bottom half on k_sys_work_q, measured at 3,568 B of its 4,096 B
	  during a live unlock, and CONFIG_LIS2DH_TRIGGER_OWN_THREAD costs a
	  1,024 B stack. This path costs one gpio_callback, one atomic_t and one
	  24 B classifier state, with no new thread and no new work queue.

	  Needs a board whose devicetree has an accel0 alias with an irq-gpios
	  property. The DWM3001CDK has both.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM_THRESHOLD_MG
	int "Impact threshold, milli-g"
	default 2000
	range 32 4000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	help
	  Transient acceleration that counts as a strike, measured after the
	  high-pass filter so gravity is not part of the budget. Quantised to
	  32 mg steps by the +/-4 g full scale the driver selects, and clamped
	  to the register's 7-bit range.

	  2000 is a PLACEHOLDER, not a recommendation. The number that matters is
	  "louder than closing this door normally", which is a property of the
	  door, its hinges and where the board is screwed. Size it from a bench
	  capture of 20 normal closes and 20 slams; if the two populations
	  overlap at every threshold, this approach is finished and the honest
	  move is to ship the angle states with no tamper signal.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM_LOG_LEVEL
	int "Log level for the impact driver"
	default 3
	range 0 4
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	help
	  Init-time only. Nothing on the interrupt path logs, and nothing can:
	  the callback runs in interrupt context beside a ranging arm deadline of
	  roughly 1836 us.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_HINGE_FRAME_MM
	int "Hinge axis to the frame anchor, millimetres"
	default 800
	range 1 5000
	help
	  Side a of the triangle ultrawidelock_door.h solves. A PLACEHOLDER, like
	  the baseline beside it in overlay-anchor.conf: the number that matters
	  is where the two boards actually ended up on this door, which is a
	  tape-measure fact and not a default anyone can ship.

	  Wrong here, every reported angle is biased and the ajar alarm fires at
	  the wrong opening. The board says so once at startup only when the
	  geometry is impossible, not when it is merely wrong.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_HINGE_LEAF_MM
	int "Hinge axis to the leaf anchor, millimetres"
	default 800
	range 1 5000
	help
	  Side b of the same triangle, and the same PLACEHOLDER status as the
	  frame distance above.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_OFFSET_MDDEG
	int "Angle the geometry reports with the door SHUT, millidegrees"
	default 0
	range 0 179999
	help
	  theta0: subtracted from every reading so a shut door reports 0.

	  A PLACEHOLDER at 0, which is what a perfectly aligned pair of anchors
	  would give and no real pair does. ultrawidelock_door.h says how to get
	  the real one: solve it from three measured points (shut, ~45, ~90
	  degrees) rather than measuring to the hinge pin, because the hinge axis
	  is not where it looks like it is.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_AJAR_DWELL_S
	int "How long the door may stand open, bolt thrown, before the ajar alarm"
	default 60
	range 1 3600
	help
	  A DoorAjar alarm (door-lock-cluster.xml AlarmCodeEnum, value 7) once
	  the leaf has been away from CLOSED for this long while the Matter node
	  still reports the bolt LOCKED.

	  60 is a PLACEHOLDER chosen the same way the impact threshold's 2000 mg
	  is not: nothing has measured how long this door is normally held open
	  during a walk-through. Long, deliberately -- the cost of guessing high
	  is a late alarm, and the cost of guessing low is an owner who learns to
	  ignore this event. Size it from the same bench session that sizes the
	  impact threshold.

endif # ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_MSG
	bool "Build the sealed-link wire codec"
	help
	  The WV2/WV3 codec alone -- no picker, no label core, no ingest. Both
	  ends of the second-anchor link need it: the lock to decode a report and
	  the satellite to encode one, and the satellite is not an ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR
	  build at all.

	  Separate from ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_CODEC because that one also carries the
	  advertiser picker, which belongs to a retired device class. A UWB anchor
	  that had to compile the BLE picker to send its own distance would be
	  exactly the coupling this split exists to remove.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_PAIR_LOG
	bool "Log this node's (ranging block, distance) for cross-anchor comparison"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_UWB
	help
	  Prints one line per accepted trusted range: the initiator's ranging
	  block and this node's distance in millimetres. Its only purpose is to
	  let two anchors' captures be JOINED ON BLOCK afterwards, so the
	  difference between them can be measured at an instant where the true
	  difference is known.

	  That difference is the question the whole two-anchor approach rests on.
	  A common-mode error -- both anchors reading long or short together --
	  cancels exactly in sign(d_inside - d_outside) and costs nothing. A
	  DIFFERENTIAL one does not cancel, and against a 60 mm dead band a
	  drifting differential error would make the side verdict meaningless. No
	  measurement taken so far can tell those two apart, because every one of
	  them came from a single anchor.

	  Printed from the grant loop on the main thread, NOT from the ranging
	  callback, and deliberately: main.c forces the per-frame trace off before
	  the radio starts because synchronous printk inside those callbacks
	  pushes the delayed Response TX past its slot. This costs that path
	  nothing.

	  Deliberately NOT dependent on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR. This instrument
	  exists to decide whether the two-anchor approach is viable at all, so
	  requiring the anchor subsystem to be compiled in before the test can run
	  would be backwards -- and it silently dropped the symbol the first time,
	  because the bench lock image does not set ANCHOR.

	  Bench instrument. Leave it off on a mounted door.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_WITNESS_PORT
	int "UDP port for the sealed peer link"
	default 49180
	range 1024 65535
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SEALED_LINK || ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_MSG
	help
	  One socket, shared by every consumer of the link: the version byte
	  demultiplexes, not the port.

	  Outside the ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR gate on purpose. BOTH ENDS of the link must
	  agree on this number, and the far end -- the satellite -- is not an
	  anchor build at all: it encodes a report and fuses nothing. A symbol
	  inside an unmet `if` is silently dropped with no warning and a clean
	  build, which is exactly how the first attempt at this failed.

	  Mesh-local only. Not registered with SRP: the witnesses are told the
	  lock's address at enrollment, and publishing a service for them would
	  advertise the lock's role to the whole fabric for no benefit.
